#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build the countertop creative CSV from LIVE Meta Ad Library data.

Data source
-----------
The session's `FB` MCP server (tool `mcp__FB__ads_library_search`), which is
backed by credentials that DO have Ad Library API access. (The raw
FACEBOOK_ACCESS_TOKEN vendored in this repo does NOT — it returns OAuthException
code 10 / subcode 2332002 until the account completes identity verification;
see COUNTERTOP_EXTRACTION.md. That path, and its script
extract_countertop_creatives.py, remain the "correct" approach once the token
is cleared.)

Each of the 7 advertisers was queried by page_id for the US, and separately for
the EU (NL for all 7, plus DE for one) to hunt for creative bodies.

What we can and cannot get
--------------------------
* The FB tool's response schema returns: id, page_name, ad_creative_link_title,
  ad_creation_time, ad_delivery_start_time, ad_snapshot_url, currency.
* It does NOT return ad_creative_bodies / link_captions / link_descriptions /
  publisher_platforms.
* Meta only populates ad_creative_bodies for ads that reached the EU. Every EU
  query (NL x7, DE x1) for these local US advertisers returned 0 ads, so there
  is no EU-reached ad from which a body could ever come.
* The public snapshot page (which shows the primary text visually) lives on
  www.facebook.com, which this environment's network policy blocks (only
  graph.facebook.com is allowlisted), so it can't be scraped here either.

Net: body_text is genuinely unavailable via the API for all of them. The ad's
headline text lives in link_title; the full visual creative is at
ad_snapshot_url. This script records that faithfully rather than pretending.

The raw tool responses are embedded verbatim below so the CSV is fully
reproducible from this file alone.
"""
import json
import csv
import os
import datetime

# --- LIVE US responses from mcp__FB__ads_library_search (page_id, ad_reached=US, status=ALL) ---
# Each value is the exact tool payload: {"results": "<json string>"}.
